description:
Flagged by ldui-zl58's implementation and confirmed here; left out of that bead because it was outside its named scope.

ldui-zl58 fixed AppShellIconNavItem by marking its bare <button> with data-pressable="true", which the drift rule (audit/src/drift.js) treats as an exemption:

  if (tag === 'BUTTON' && !el.classList.contains('btn')) {
    if (!el.closest(EXEMPT_CLOSEST) && !el.hasAttribute('data-pressable')) { push(...) }
  }

NavRailItem (src/components/nav_rail/component.rs:230, class from style.rs item_class) has the IDENTICAL shape and no marker:

  "relative flex h-12 w-12 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-box ..."

which renders as button.relative.flex - the same selector the 4iiz-etl audit reported for the app-shell rail. So a consumer that uses NavRail rather than AppShell's own rail still gets the full run of button-without-btn findings, and ldui-zl58's fix does nothing for them. The two rails should not disagree about whether an icon rail item is a deliberate unstyled action.

SCOPE: apply the same decision to NavRailItem, and check whether any other library-owned bare <button> has the same exposure (grep for `<button` across src/ and check each for .btn, data-pressable, or an EXEMPT_CLOSEST ancestor). Worth doing as one sweep rather than one component at a time, since the whole point is that OUR markup should not generate findings in a consumer's audit.

SEPARATE OBSERVATION while reading that file, worth confirming rather than assuming: nav_rail/style.rs:34 uses `text-base-content/60` for the inactive item. This crate's convention is muted copy at /75, and the AA pass that accompanied the Pressable/TableViewport work found 60%-and-below muted text failing contrast (the opacity-60 and opacity-50 variants were replaced for exactly that reason). This is the alpha-mix form rather than an opacity utility, so it is not automatically the same defect - but it is the same ratio, on text, in a component every screen carries. Measure it before changing it.
